            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "AI tends to put these \"used to do x\" comments in , and it actually can be more confusing for the future. A human would rarely do that, it's a distraction and for spelunking we have git logs. And the test also serves as documentation.\n\n```suggestion\n      -- Self-delimiting, collision-free encoding: `n` for nil, else `<len>:<content>`.\n```",
            "commit_id": "d5e63324203e547f456835315fd120a64652192f",
            "created_at": "2026-07-12T11:33:36Z",
            "diff_hunk": "@@ -6,7 +6,25 @@\n --- @return fun(...): any\n local function concat_hash(argc)\n   return function(...)\n-    return table.concat({ ... }, '%%', 1, argc)\n+    --- @type any[]\n+    local args = { ... }\n+    local n = argc or select('#', ...)\n+    --- @type string[]\n+    local parts = {}\n+    for i = 1, n do\n+      -- Use a self-delimiting, collision-free encoding: `n` for nil, `<len>:<content>`\n+      -- otherwise. A plain `table.concat` with a fixed separator collides when an\n+      -- argument contains the separator (e.g. (\"a\",\"%b\") and (\"a%\",\"b\") both hashed to\n+      -- \"a%%%b\").",
